has with a swath of nature.

00:40:20.830 --> 00:40:21.663
Hmm.

00:40:21.663 --> 00:40:24.417
And maybe this is akin to
the lawn mowing thing, too,

00:40:24.417 --> 00:40:25.570
"Oh, the grass is thicker
at this time of year."

00:40:25.570 --> 00:40:27.400
You can't mow in a national park.

00:40:28.770 --> 00:40:29.970
There are certain places where...

00:40:29.970 --> 00:40:32.080
I've seen weed eaters in a national park.

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:34.155
But not the native grasses.

00:40:34.155 --> 00:40:35.235
You can't be cutting the native grasses.

00:40:35.235 --> 00:40:36.870
Can't be cut. Yeah, you're right.

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:37.703
You're right.

00:40:37.703 --> 00:40:38.536
But then I'm like,

00:40:38.536 --> 00:40:41.400
"Maybe I'm just jealous
of one of those hikers

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:45.357
that does the Appalachian Trail,"

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:48.180
having the time to do that.

00:40:48.180 --> 00:40:52.190
So I'm trying to settle on
where my jealousy really lies,

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:53.238
and I don't think it's...

00:40:53.238 --> 00:40:54.440
I think it's less of an occupation

00:40:54.440 --> 00:40:58.170
and more of a connection with nature.

00:40:58.170 --> 00:41:01.253
Well, I think that the
hiking the Appalachian Trail,

00:41:02.620 --> 00:41:03.453
I mean,

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:06.580
I still feel like we
should do it at one point.

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:08.392
I mean, it takes a long time.

00:41:08.392 --> 00:41:10.600
Not necessarily the whole thing.

00:41:10.600 --> 00:41:12.550
I mean, you're talking about isolation,

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:13.790
it's like that could be an insult

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:17.340
to the loved ones you live with.

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:18.566
Oh, they understand.

00:41:18.566 --> 00:41:19.399
It's like,

00:41:19.399 --> 00:41:23.600
"Okay, so you're so jealous
that you can't get away from me,

00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:25.493
can't get away from us."

00:41:25.493 --> 00:41:27.630
But there is something...

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:30.040
I was gonna say sexy but
there's nothing sexy about it.

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:33.060
There's something intriguing about it,

00:41:33.060 --> 00:41:34.110
adventurous.

00:41:34.110 --> 00:41:35.470
I mean, I've always had

00:41:39.165 --> 00:41:42.667
this secret dream to do something like,

00:41:42.667 --> 00:41:44.060
"Okay, yeah,

00:41:44.060 --> 00:41:47.310
I am gonna hike the
Appalachian Trail this year."

00:41:47.310 --> 00:41:48.390
Or the sailboat of it all

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:51.447
is something that you love the idea.

00:41:51.447 --> 00:41:54.130
None of that is practical at this point

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:56.647
not just because of relationships,

00:41:56.647 --> 00:41:58.373
but just position in life.

00:41:58.373 --> 00:42:00.238
It's like, okay, we can't do that.

00:42:00.238 --> 00:42:01.660
If we're gonna do something like that,

00:42:01.660 --> 00:42:02.493
we have to be like...

00:42:02.493 --> 00:42:03.326
Well, we're gonna make it look

00:42:03.326 --> 00:42:04.159
like we hiked the Appalachian Trail,

00:42:04.159 --> 00:42:05.140
and we're gonna shoot it in a week,

00:42:05.140 --> 00:42:06.330
and it's gonna be a YouTube video.

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:07.163
You know?

00:42:07.163 --> 00:42:08.760
It's like...

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:11.310
But we should do...

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:16.870
I mean, I think we should
set a date in the future.

00:42:16.870 --> 00:42:17.703
And it should be like,

00:42:17.703 --> 00:42:19.090
"By this time,

00:42:19.090 --> 00:42:22.550
you have to have made plans
to do something like that,"

00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:24.730
whether we do it together or...

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:26.960
Because I just think that
before you get too old,

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:29.090
where you can't do
something adventurous...

00:42:29.090 --> 00:42:31.560
Like a lot of people do the shorter...

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:34.660
They hike that trail in the...

00:42:34.660 --> 00:42:35.850
Is it the one that goes from...

00:42:35.850 --> 00:42:37.380
It's in Spain,

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:38.630
and it goes

00:42:39.649 --> 00:42:42.450
to a bunch of like-
I don't know that.

00:42:42.450 --> 00:42:43.283
Oh, I know what you're talking about.

00:42:43.283 --> 00:42:44.260
Churches or something.

00:42:44.260 --> 00:42:47.400
It's a famous quest that people go on.

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:48.470
I mean, it does remind me

00:42:48.470 --> 00:42:51.674
that we do need to get solo trips

00:42:51.674 --> 00:42:53.640
on the books again.

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:54.473
That's gotta happen again.

00:42:54.473 --> 00:42:56.800
I keep trying to find that time.

00:42:56.800 --> 00:43:01.160
My number seven is not unrelated
to what we're talking about

00:43:01.160 --> 00:43:02.890
'cause it's Jared Diamond.

00:43:02.890 --> 00:43:05.270
So Jared Diamond is..

00:43:05.270 --> 00:43:06.327
He wrote one of my favorite books,

00:43:06.327 --> 00:43:08.370
"Guns, Germs, and Steel," right?

00:43:08.370 --> 00:43:09.980
So he's basically...

00:43:11.010 --> 00:43:13.150
He's also old, he's 84.

00:43:13.150 --> 00:43:15.050
I'm just jealous of old...

00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:16.710
I'm not jealous of...

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:18.480
I don't wanna be 84 right now

00:43:20.140 --> 00:43:21.980
but I'm just jealous of how he got

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:24.330
from where he was

00:43:24.330 --> 00:43:25.390
to how he got to 84

00:43:26.326 --> 00:43:28.040
and what he's done in between.

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:31.497
So this guy has written
"The Third Chimpanzee,"

00:43:31.497 --> 00:43:34.227
"Guns, Germs, and Steel," "Collapse,"

00:43:34.227 --> 00:43:38.620
"The World Until
Yesterday," these sort of...

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:43.830
They're historical/scientific explorations

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:45.533
of really interesting things.

00:43:47.330 --> 00:43:50.130
I mean, obviously he's like
a professor at the same time,

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:53.660
but he's just a guy who
has traveled the world

00:43:53.660 --> 00:43:58.650
and been on places like
Easter Island or New Guinea.

00:43:58.650 --> 00:43:59.650
He's a bird watcher,

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:02.280
which that's not something
that I necessarily aspire to,

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:06.860
but his bird watching
and his interest in...

00:44:07.900 --> 00:44:10.680
His ornithology, is that what you call it?

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:11.535
Took him to...

00:44:11.535 --> 00:44:13.300
He would take trips to New Guinea

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:17.470
to discover and observe birds.

00:44:17.470 --> 00:44:18.303
So just somebody

00:44:18.303 --> 00:44:23.250
who's sort of like as close
to a modern day Indiana Jones

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:25.533
is kinda what I'm trying to find.

00:44:25.533 --> 00:44:28.270
He's like an explorer/writer.

00:44:28.270 --> 00:44:29.960
Yeah. So, it's-

00:44:29.960 --> 00:44:32.677
Like traveling in order
to then find something out

00:44:32.677 --> 00:44:34.680
that then you can write about

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:36.123
that a lot of people
will read and be like,

00:44:36.123 --> 00:44:38.557
"Man, this guy really
put it all together."

00:44:39.687 --> 00:44:43.960
The travel and the adventure
mixed with the knowledge

00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:45.000
that you're getting

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:49.780
and then synthesizing of the knowledge

00:44:49.780 --> 00:44:52.280
into a work,

00:44:52.280 --> 00:44:54.990
all those things are
super intriguing to me.

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:58.880
And so, you know...

00:45:00.530 --> 00:45:02.027
At this point in my life, I can be like,

00:45:02.027 --> 00:45:03.923
"Well, I can also travel,

00:45:05.440 --> 00:45:07.590
I can also watch birds,"

00:45:07.590 --> 00:45:09.703
but I'm not trained.

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:12.420
If I'm gonna write a book,

00:45:12.420 --> 00:45:15.917
it's gonna be like "The Book of Mythical."

00:45:17.055 --> 00:45:18.467
It's not gonna be-

00:45:18.467 --> 00:45:20.027
I actually think you wouldn't write

00:45:20.027 --> 00:45:21.090
"Germs, Guns, and Steel,"

00:45:21.090 --> 00:45:22.420
I think it would be fiction

00:45:22.420 --> 00:45:24.400
but it would still be travel related,

00:45:24.400 --> 00:45:27.490
and you need to justify a lot of research.

00:45:27.490 --> 00:45:31.840
I think that could be like your swan song.

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:35.680
Well, and I think that
there's something to...

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:39.410
He's done, a bunch of stuff

00:45:39.410 --> 00:45:40.740
and he's obviously stayed really busy,

00:45:40.740 --> 00:45:45.040
but then it'd be like
this particular quest

00:45:45.040 --> 00:45:47.810
resulted in this particular
book, and it's like:

00:45:47.810 --> 00:45:50.330
How many years of thinking about...

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:52.277
'Cause "Collapse" is really, really cool,

00:45:52.277 --> 00:45:53.457
the 2005 book,

00:45:53.457 --> 00:45:56.500
and it uses the story of Easter Island

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:57.340
and how Easter Island

00:45:57.340 --> 00:45:59.690
basically decimated their own population

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:00.840
and their natural resources

00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:02.246
because of an ideology
that they were following

00:46:02.246 --> 00:46:04.022
with putting the statues up.

00:46:04.022 --> 00:46:04.855
Huh.

00:46:04.855 --> 00:46:05.770
And he uses that as an analogy

00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:09.180
to talk about what we're
doing as a culture,

00:46:09.180 --> 00:46:11.660
with our commitment to consumerism

00:46:11.660 --> 00:46:15.110
and what the results of the impact

00:46:15.110 --> 00:46:16.940
on the environment will be.

00:46:16.940 --> 00:46:19.250
And so on this larger scale,
we're doing what they did;

00:46:19.250 --> 00:46:21.290
but it's like: How much...

00:46:21.290 --> 00:46:23.330
I wanna go to Easter Island,
but I don't wanna go...

00:46:23.330 --> 00:46:24.980
He doesn't go to Easter Island

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:28.980
with a cool backpack
he got off of Instagram

00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:32.550
so he can take pictures
that he puts on Instagram,

00:46:32.550 --> 00:46:34.220
and that's the end of it,

00:46:34.220 --> 00:46:36.607
which is what I would probably do, right?

00:46:36.607 --> 00:46:39.360
Like, "I took my film
camera to Easter Island.

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:42.480
I got some cool-ass film
pictures of these statues.

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:44.090
Here's my carousel on Instagram.

00:46:44.090 --> 00:46:44.923
Isn't this cool?"

00:46:44.923 --> 00:46:46.920
"Oh man, you got an eye for photography."

00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:49.040
And that's the end of it, right?

00:46:49.040 --> 00:46:50.820
It's like, no, no.

00:46:50.820 --> 00:46:52.030
This guy goes there

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:54.900
and he's coming to
conclusions about things

00:46:54.900 --> 00:46:58.620
and making statements about things

00:46:58.620 --> 00:47:01.310
and actually impacting the
way that we're moving forward

00:47:01.310 --> 00:47:02.400
as a civilization.

00:47:02.400 --> 00:47:05.290
He's not just trying to
get likes on Instagram.

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:06.673
You know what I mean?

00:47:08.060 --> 00:47:10.560
I don't, but I know that you do.

00:47:10.560 --> 00:47:12.240
I am jealous of Jared Diamond.

00:47:12.240 --> 00:47:14.023
He's not going on my list, but I get that.

00:47:14.023 --> 00:47:15.330
I think that's totally you.

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:19.280
Now, this is one that I
think we've talked about

00:47:19.280 --> 00:47:22.580
that if it's not on your list,

00:47:22.580 --> 00:47:23.610
you're gonna put it on there

00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:25.130
because we've had this discussion

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:28.543
about being jealous of this duo:

00:47:30.310 --> 00:47:32.010
the Coen Brothers.
Okay.

00:47:32.010 --> 00:47:34.690
We talk specifically about...

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:36.680
First of all, they're filmmakers.

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:39.000
They have built their brand,

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:42.507
their identity on their amazing
films that they just say,

00:47:42.507 --> 00:47:43.890
"You know what?"

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:44.987
And then we say things like,

00:47:44.987 --> 00:47:49.210
"Man, they conceptualize this
film and then they just...

00:47:49.210 --> 00:47:51.720
they get in a room and they beat it out.

00:47:51.720 --> 00:47:55.670
And then there's a couch in
there and they'll leave it...

00:47:55.670 --> 00:47:59.020
Some days they'll just sleep on the couch

00:47:59.020 --> 00:48:02.510
and then wake up and keep
writing and storyboarding,

00:48:02.510 --> 00:48:04.330
meticulously planning everything,"

00:48:04.330 --> 00:48:09.330
that everybody gives them full
trust and creative freedom

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:11.560
to just do their thing;

00:48:11.560 --> 00:48:15.744
and then every few years or
so, another one comes out.

00:48:15.744 --> 00:48:18.610
And so that creative experience,

00:48:18.610 --> 00:48:20.180
especially in the world of film

00:48:20.180 --> 00:48:23.190
that was always this aspiration of ours

00:48:23.190 --> 00:48:25.070
that we have not achieved,

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:29.380
is something that manifests as jealousy

00:48:29.380 --> 00:48:31.290
when you hear about their process.

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:33.860
And I have somebody on my list later

00:48:35.090 --> 00:48:37.073
for the same exact reasons.
Okay.

00:48:39.240 --> 00:48:41.718
But I think the film part of it,

00:48:41.718 --> 00:48:43.777
I'm jealous of that too.

00:48:43.777 --> 00:48:45.360
It's like, oh,

00:48:45.360 --> 00:48:46.514
conceptualizing

00:48:46.514 --> 00:48:47.903
a world meticulously.
That's what I'm saying.

00:48:47.903 --> 00:48:50.020
I have another filmmaker
for the same reason.

00:48:50.020 --> 00:48:52.960
I think that there is...

00:48:57.550 --> 00:48:58.383
It's funny.

00:48:58.383 --> 00:48:59.216
We had that conversation

00:48:59.216 --> 00:49:01.300
with Matthew McConaughey on this show.

00:49:01.300 --> 00:49:02.430
Remember that?

00:49:02.430 --> 00:49:06.230
And we kind of went
into getting his advice,

00:49:06.230 --> 00:49:07.320
which he likes to give advice,

00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:09.553
that's kind of what
"Greenlights" is all about.

00:49:11.360 --> 00:49:13.530
And then we were basically talking

00:49:13.530 --> 00:49:15.567
about our desire to create, like,

00:49:15.567 --> 00:49:17.580
"We feel like we're being held back."

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:18.607
And then he said something like,

00:49:18.607 --> 00:49:20.350
"You don't need to ask for permission."

00:49:20.350 --> 00:49:24.283
And it's like, yeah, I understand that,

00:49:26.470 --> 00:49:29.030
but unfortunately we still
kind of are in a place

00:49:29.030 --> 00:49:31.193
where there is a certain
level of permission

00:49:31.193 --> 00:49:33.323
that we have to receive, right?

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:37.500
You think about when
you wanna create things

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:39.510
on a certain level...

00:49:39.510 --> 00:49:40.700
Sure, we can do whatever we want to

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:41.960
on the internet within reason

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:44.563
if we can mobilize our team to do it,

00:49:45.910 --> 00:49:49.860
but it has to be very economical

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:51.440
in order for us to be able to pull it off,

00:49:51.440 --> 00:49:52.933
both in time in and money.

00:49:53.780 --> 00:49:54.613
And so I think the thing

00:49:54.613 --> 00:49:57.327
that you're tapping
into is somebody's like,

00:49:57.327 --> 00:50:01.910
"Hey, here's plenty of money

00:50:01.910 --> 00:50:02.980
to do something

00:50:02.980 --> 00:50:04.793
where we're not gonna come
back necessarily and say,

00:50:04.793 --> 00:50:07.710
'You can't do that because of money.'"

00:50:08.650 --> 00:50:11.060
I'm not saying they get to
do whatever they want to.

00:50:11.060 --> 00:50:13.970
They're not spending like 500 million

00:50:13.970 --> 00:50:14.940
on their movies or whatever.

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:17.210
They're still making
movies that are reasonable.

00:50:17.210 --> 00:50:20.900
But no one is coming along...

00:50:20.900 --> 00:50:23.593
Even what we did with
"Inside Eats," right?

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:27.180
Proud of the show that we created,

00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:31.280
but we're creating it for another
audience in a lot of ways,

00:50:31.280 --> 00:50:32.113
we're creating it for...

00:50:32.113 --> 00:50:35.030
it's gotta work on Food Network, right?

00:50:35.030 --> 00:50:36.497
Whereas they're just like,

00:50:36.497 --> 00:50:38.700
"I'm making a movie that's just gonna work

00:50:38.700 --> 00:50:41.520
for anyone who wants to see
the movie that we're making.

00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:44.240
And we're not getting permission

00:50:44.240 --> 00:50:46.290
from anybody creatively."

00:50:46.290 --> 00:50:48.570
And you think about the buy-in they get.

00:50:48.570 --> 00:50:52.543
Any talent they want is
clamoring to work with them,

00:50:52.543 --> 00:50:54.030
it's an automatic yes.

00:50:54.030 --> 00:50:55.040
Give me your next one.

00:50:55.040 --> 00:50:56.708
Number six, John Mayer.

00:50:56.708 --> 00:50:58.900
I wanted to put a musician on the list.

00:50:58.900 --> 00:50:59.733
Okay.

00:50:59.733 --> 00:51:02.320
And I'm sure I could have...

00:51:03.520 --> 00:51:06.550
He's not like the end-all-be-all
I'm jealous of him

00:51:06.550 --> 00:51:09.180
in particular more so than anybody else,

00:51:09.180 --> 00:51:11.620
but I think that there is a...

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:13.880
Like, I didn't put Harry Styles on here

